/-!
# The sheaf-theoretic décalage of an invertible ideal and its stalks

Let `X` be a ringed space, `I` an invertible sheaf of `O_X`-modules and `ι : I ⟶ O_X` a
monomorphism exhibiting `I` as an invertible ideal sheaf. For a cochain complex `F` of
`I`-torsion-free `O_X`-modules the *décalage* `η_I F` is the complex whose degree-`n` term is
the fibre product
`I^{⊗ n} ⊗ F^n ×_{I^{⊗ n} ⊗ F^{n+1}} I^{⊗ (n+1)} ⊗ F^{n+1}`, the differential being the one
induced by `d^n`. At a point `x` of `X` at which the stalk ideal `I_x ⊆ O_{X,x}` is generated
by `f`, the stalk of `η_I F` is the ordinary décalage `η_f` of the complex of stalks, in the
sense of More on Algebra, Tag 0F7N.

## Main definitions

* `idealPowerTerm`, `idealPowerMap`: the integer tensor power `I^{⊗ n} ⊗ -` of an invertible
  ideal, on objects and on morphisms.
* `idealPowerIncl`: the transition `I^{⊗ (n+1)} ⊗ M ⟶ I^{⊗ n} ⊗ M` induced by `ι`.
* `etaTerm`, `EtaDifferentialData`, `etaDifferentialData`, `etaObj`: the degreewise fibre
  product, the data pinning down the induced differential, and the resulting complex `η_I F`.
* `stalkComplex`, `stalkModuleFunctor`: the degreewise stalk of a complex of sheaves of
  modules, as a complex of modules over the commutative local ring `O_{X,x}`.
* `IdealPowerStalkComparison`, `idealPowerStalkComparison`: the comparison identifying the
  stalk of `I^{⊗ n} ⊗ M` with the submodule `f^{-n} M_x` of the localization `(M_x)_f`.
* `etaStalkIso`: the canonical isomorphism between the stalk of `η_I F` and `η_f` of the
  stalk complex.

## Main results

* `etaStalkIso` (Tag 0GT6) identifies `(η_I F)_x` with `η_f (F_x)` at a generator `f` of `I_x`.
* `etaStalkIso_hom` characterizes it degreewise: composed with the inclusion of the
  décalage term into the localization, it is the normalized ideal-power comparison applied to
  the first projection of the defining fibre product.
* `IdealPowerStalkComparison.map_eq`: an `IdealPowerStalkComparison` is unique, so the
  normalization used by `etaStalkIso_hom` involves no choice beyond that of `f`.
* `idealPowerIncl_mono` and `etaTerm_snd_comp_d_eq_zero`: the two facts about an
  `I`-torsion-free complex that make the differential of `η_I F` well defined.

## Implementation notes

Situation 0GT4 asks that `I` be locally generated by a single nonzerodivisor, which is
condition (1) of Tag 0GT3. What is assumed here is instead condition (2) of that tag, namely
that `I` is invertible together with `Mono ι`; this is formally weaker, hence the statements
below are formally stronger, and the generalization is the one sanctioned by the footnote to
Situation 0GT4 ("The discussion in this section can be generalized to the case where all we
require is that `I` is an invertible `O_X`-module").

The two descriptions of `(η_I F)^n` given in the source — the kernel of
`⟨d^n, -1⟩ : I^n F^n ⊕ I^{n+1} F^{n+1} → I^n F^{n+1}` and the fibre product of `I^n F^n` and
`I^{n+1} F^{n+1}` over `I^n F^{n+1}` — are the same object, and it is the fibre product that
is used here, as `etaTerm`.

The integer powers are the iterates of the tensor autoequivalence of Tag 01CU, in the
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.Invertible` spelling: that development carries the tensor product
bifunctor and its right unitor, which is what `idealPowerIncl` is built from. Invertibility is
taken in the `Stacks.SheafOfModules.IsInvertible` spelling of Tag 01CS. The spelling
`AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.IsInvertible` that development expects is an abbreviation of
it, so the hypothesis is used directly, with no bridging instance.

`EtaComplex` is a development about modules over a *commutative* ring, while
`AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.stalkFunctor` produces modules over the `RingCat`-valued
stalk. The two are bridged by `SemilinearStalk.commStalkIso`, so `stalkModuleFunctor` is the
stalk functor followed by restriction of scalars along it; this is why the stalk complex is
built here rather than reusing `AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.stalkComplex`, which lands
over the `RingCat` stalk.

A generator `f` of `I_x` is used only to describe the local identification: it turns the
abstract twist `I^{⊗ n} ⊗ F` into the concrete submodule `f^{-n} F_x` of the localization
`(F_x)_f` over which `EtaComplex.term` is defined. The generator enters the *data* of
`etaStalkIso` through `idealPowerStalkComparison` alone, and that comparison is unique by
`IdealPowerStalkComparison.map_eq`. The generator is never *chosen*: the preimage of `f` in
`I_x` is not picked out of an existence statement but obtained as the unique preimage under
the injection `ι_x`, which is what `idealGeneratorMap` is.

`etaStalkIso` and `idealPowerStalkComparison` are built from private auxiliary declarations, so
their bodies are not exposed; they are pinned down instead by `etaStalkIso_hom` and by the
fields of `IdealPowerStalkComparison` together with `IdealPowerStalkComparison.map_eq`.

Two spellings of the structure sheaf of rings of `X` are in play — `structureSheaf X.sheaf`,
which the invertibility and tensor-power calculus uses, and
`structureSheafRing X`, which the stalk calculus
uses, with `Stacks.SheafOfModules.TensorSheafification.structureSheaf X.sheaf.obj _` a third.
All three are the same sheaf of rings, but identifying the first with the others unfolds
`TopCat.Sheaf` and `CategoryTheory.sheafCompose`, neither of which unfolds below `default`
transparency; the identification is therefore unavailable to unification on implicit and
instance-implicit arguments, which is checked at `implicit` transparency. The public
statements below mix the spellings unavoidably — `IsIdealTorsionFree X ι M` and
`grpStalk X M.val x` are indexed by the stalk spelling while `M` is a module over the first.

Two devices carry the private proofs across the identification. Where instance search is
what fails, the missing instance is declared by hand at the spelling the statement uses,
with the upstream instance as its body; `moduleGrpStalk`, `moduleRestrictScalarsSection`,
`moduleTensorStalkSections` and `moduleRingCatStalk` are all of that
kind. Where a *rewrite* is what fails — `rw` unifies explicit arguments at `instances`
transparency and `simp`'s discrimination trees match at `reducible`, below which none of the
three spellings unfold — the step is written out as a chain of ground equations instead: the
goal is put in a single spelling with `change`, each lemma is supplied with
explicit arguments, and the chain is closed with `Eq.trans`, `congrArg` and `exact`, where
the check runs at `default` transparency.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0GT6](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GT6)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0GT4](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GT4)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0GT5](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GT5)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0F7N](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0F7N)
-/

